  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186115299,
      "id": "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac",
      "title": "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "30dd55ed-b006-5bc8-becc-3514962e12bd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y &=-2 \\\\ x-y &=-6 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2}y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system has no solution\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system is consistent dependent",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & \u2194{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 16 \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 16 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=-6 \\\\ y &=16.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=16.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-(16)=-6\\,\u27f9\\,x=10.\n$$\n\n \nSo,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent.   \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "6ae2b280-5469-50d3-9fb7-b2306c635502",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "46cd253c-f2f2-583e-81b4-59848c664df6",
